, and e-commerce and digital platforms. The company is based in Tampa, Florida.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Eric J. Foss | CEO, Principal Operating Officer & Executive Chairman | Age 67 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 12K people. Revenue per employee of $555K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Defensive peers. Each employee generates $25K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Primo Brands Corporation is a mid-cap consumer defensive business with a $8.4B market cap on $6.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +29% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 31%.","The business throws off $303M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 5 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 11.32\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 4.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.3\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 6.7% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.2) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated 